# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The account before it. Luke 16 opens with another account: “And he said also unto his disciples, There was a certain rich man, which had a steward” (16:1). It runs to 16:13 and closes with “Ye cannot serve God and mammon” (16:13). It is told to the disciples, and Luke says so at 16:1.'))
A(('p', '2.2 The turn to the Pharisees. Luke then writes, “And the Pharisees also, who were covetous, heard all these things: and they derided him” (16:14). Jesus answers them: “Ye are they which justify yourselves before men; but God knoweth your hearts: for that which is highly esteemed among men is abomination in the sight of God” (16:15). Luke does not mark a change of speaker or of hearers between 16:18 and 16:19. Verse 19 begins with no “he said” and names no one who is listening.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The sayings between. After the answer to the Pharisees Luke records three sayings. “The law and the prophets were until John: since that time the kingdom of God is preached, and every man presseth into it” (16:16). “And it is easier for heaven and earth to pass, than one tittle of the law to fail” (16:17). Then a saying about putting away a wife and marrying another (16:18). Luke does not say how these three sayings are joined to 16:19. The words “the law and the prophets” stand at 16:16, and Abraham’s words “Moses and the prophets” stand at 16:29 and 16:31. This study reports the two wordings and does not say that one explains the other.'))
A(('p', '2.4 No label on the account. Luke 16:19 begins, “There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple and fine linen, and fared sumptuously every day.” The word “parable” does not occur in Luke 16, and Luke does not say whether the account is a parable or a narrative of events. This study does not decide that, and it adds nothing to what Luke gives.'))
A(('p', '2.5 The prophetic context. Luke has said what Jesus preached: “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). He sent the Twelve, “And he sent them to preach the kingdom of God, and to heal the sick” (9:2). At 16:16 the saying is, “since that time the kingdom of God is preached.” The Pharisees hear and deride (16:14), and the disciples are addressed at 16:1 and again at 17:1: “Then said he unto the disciples, It is impossible but that offences will come” (17:1).'))
A(('p', '2.6 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives no statement of why Jesus told this account. What Luke gives is the place after the steward (16:1–13); the Pharisees who were covetous and derided Him (16:14); His answer to them (16:15); the three sayings (16:16–18); and the turn to the disciples at 17:1. The account is not a free-floating saying about riches or about death in general. It stands in a chapter of Luke, and it is read there.'))
A(('call', 'No Question or Label Introduces This Account',
   'No question from a hearer comes before it, and no comparison opens it. It does not begin “the kingdom is like.” Luke does not call it a parable and does not say it records events. It begins with “There was a certain rich man” (16:19). This study adds nothing to that.'))
