# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The account is read here in the order Jesus gives it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another passage, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The rich man (16:19). “There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple and fine linen, and fared sumptuously every day.” The account begins with a certain rich man. It says three things of him: he was clothed in purple and fine linen, and he fared sumptuously every day. It does not give his name.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The beggar (16:20–21). “And there was a certain beggar named Lazarus, which was laid at his gate, full of sores, And desiring to be fed with the crumbs which fell from the rich man’s table: moreover the dogs came and licked his sores.” A certain beggar is named Lazarus. He was laid at the rich man’s gate, full of sores, desiring to be fed with the crumbs from the rich man’s table, and the dogs came and licked his sores. The text says he was laid at the gate. It does not say that the rich man saw him, spoke to him or refused him.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The two deaths (16:22). “And it came to pass, that the beggar died, and was carried by the angels into Abraham’s bosom: the rich man also died, and was buried.” Both die. Of the beggar the text says he was carried by the angels into Abraham’s bosom. Of the rich man it says only that he also died and was buried.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The rich man in torments (16:23). “And in hell he lift up his eyes, being in torments, and seeth Abraham afar off, and Lazarus in his bosom.” The KJV prints “hell.” The original word in this verse is Hades, and the KJV’s word is the translators’ choice. The verse says the rich man lifts up his eyes, is in torments, and sees Abraham afar off and Lazarus in his bosom.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The first request (16:24). “And he cried and said, Father Abraham, have mercy on me, and send Lazarus, that he may dip the tip of his finger in water, and cool my tongue; for I am tormented in this flame.” The rich man speaks. He calls Abraham “Father,” asks for mercy, asks that Lazarus be sent with water for his tongue, and gives the reason: “for I am tormented in this flame.”'))
A(('p', '3.6 Abraham answers (16:25–26). “But Abraham said, Son, remember that thou in thy lifetime receivedst thy good things, and likewise Lazarus evil things: but now he is comforted, and thou art tormented. And beside all this, between us and you there is a great gulf fixed: so that they which would pass from hence to you cannot; neither can they pass to us, that would come from thence.” Abraham answers in two parts. First he sets the lifetime beside the present: good things and evil things then, comfort and torment now. Then he says a great gulf is fixed between them, and that no one can pass from one side to the other.'))
A(('p', '3.7 The second request (16:27–28). “Then he said, I pray thee therefore, father, that thou wouldest send him to my father’s house: For I have five brethren; that he may testify unto them, lest they also come into this place of torment.” The rich man asks again. This time he asks that Lazarus be sent to his father’s house, where he has five brethren, to testify to them “lest they also come into this place of torment.”'))
A(('p', '3.8 Abraham’s second answer (16:29). “Abraham saith unto him, They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them.” The answer is one sentence. It names what the five brethren have and tells them to hear it.'))
A(('p', '3.9 The rich man’s reply (16:30). “And he said, Nay, father Abraham: but if one went unto them from the dead, they will repent.” The rich man says no. He says that if one went to them from the dead, they will repent.'))
A(('p', '3.10 The last word (16:31). “And he said unto him, If they hear not Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded, though one rose from the dead.” These are the last words of the account. Luke does not say what the rich man answered, or whether anyone was sent. The next verse in Luke begins a new address to the disciples (17:1).'))
