A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring studies. The Shrewd Manager, the Rich Fool and the Lost Son are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. The Digital Study The Realms of the Dead (Study 79) reads the words for the unseen world in their own passages. This account makes no use of their meanings, and they make none of this one.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus tells of a certain rich man who fared sumptuously every day and a beggar named Lazarus laid at his gate. Both die. Lazarus is carried by the angels into Abraham’s bosom, and the rich man, in hell, in torments, asks for mercy and for Lazarus to be sent. Abraham answers with the lifetime of good things and evil things, with comfort and torment, and with a great gulf fixed. The rich man asks about his five brethren, and Abraham says they have Moses and the prophets and that if they do not hear them, they will not be persuaded though one rose from the dead. No question and no comparison opens the account, and Jesus gives no explanation of it or of any part of it.'))
